+A one-to-one relationship is declared with the principal key on the parent and the unique foreign key on the child. **Uniqueness of the foreign key is trusted, not validated**: nextorm reads no schema or index metadata, so backing the foreign key with a unique index/constraint on the dependent side is the caller's responsibility, and the declaration itself is accepted even when the data repeats. A genuinely non-unique foreign key is caught only at runtime, and only when the dependent entity exposes a non-null identity (a mapped key): `JoinInto` materialization throws `InvalidOperationException` when a parent matches more than one child with a distinct identity, a repeat of the same identity is tolerated, and a **keyless** dependent has no identity to compare — the first matched child is kept and no exception is thrown (see below):
 
